// Fakta Malaysia: one is shown per day on the Kalendar app.
// Fields: cat (category, one of the fixed list), t (the fact, English), on (optional "MM-DD": pin to that date every year)
window.KALENDAR_FACTS = [
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Sejarah
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "On 31 August 1957, Tunku Abdul Rahman stood before the crowd at Stadium Merdeka in Kuala Lumpur and raised his fist to cry 'Merdeka!' seven times. Thousands of voices roared back each time, and the Federation of Malaya was independent at last.", on: "08-31" },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Malaysia was born on 16 September 1963, when Malaya joined with Sabah, Sarawak and Singapore to form a new federation. Singapore went its own way in 1965, and since 2010 Malaysia Day has been a public holiday across the whole country.", on: "09-16" },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "On 20 February 1956, Tunku Abdul Rahman returned from independence talks in London and shared the good news at Padang Pahlawan in Bandar Hilir, Melaka. Malaya would be free the following year, and the huge crowd erupted in cries of 'Merdeka!'", on: "02-20" },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "On 22 July 1963, Sarawak was granted self-government, a few weeks before it joined in forming Malaysia. Since 2016, 22 July has been celebrated as a public holiday in Sarawak.", on: "07-22" },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Kuala Lumpur was granted city status on 1 February 1972 and became a Federal Territory on 1 February 1974. Putrajaya became a Federal Territory on the very same date in 2001, which is why 1 February is celebrated as Hari Wilayah.", on: "02-01" },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "On 10 October 2007, Dr Sheikh Muszaphar Shukor blasted off aboard a Soyuz spacecraft from Baikonur in Kazakhstan, becoming the first Malaysian in space. He spent over a week on the International Space Station, carrying out scientific experiments.", on: "10-10" },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "The Proton Saga, Malaysia's first national car, rolled out in 1985 and soon became a familiar sight on roads across the country. Its name, picked through a public naming contest, is shared with the biji saga, the shiny red seed generations of children have collected." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "The Melaka Sultanate, founded around 1400 by a prince named Parameswara, grew into one of the great trading ports of Asia. Ships rode the monsoon winds into its harbour bringing silk, porcelain, cloth and spices from China, India, Arabia and the islands." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Melaka changed hands several times: the Portuguese captured it in 1511, the Dutch took over in 1641, and it passed to the British in the 1820s. Each left its mark, from old fortress walls to the red buildings of the Dutch Square." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Captain Francis Light set up a British trading post on Penang in 1786 and named it Prince of Wales Island. Legend says he fired silver coins from a cannon into the jungle to encourage workers to clear the land faster." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Perak Man, an almost complete human skeleton roughly 10,000 to 11,000 years old, was found in 1991 in a cave in Perak's Lenggong Valley. He had been carefully buried in a curled-up position with stone tools, and the valley became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2012." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "In 1958, archaeologists working in the Niah Caves of Sarawak unearthed a human skull later dated to about 40,000 years ago. Nicknamed the 'Deep Skull', it was at the time the oldest modern human remains known in Southeast Asia." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "The Bujang Valley in Kedah is dotted with the ruins of ancient brick temples called candi, along with traces of riverside iron-working and trading settlements more than a thousand years old. It is one of the most important archaeological sites in Malaysia." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Kuala Lumpur began around 1857, when tin prospectors made their way up the Klang River to the point where it meets the Gombak. The rough little mining settlement that grew there went on to become the nation's capital." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Yap Ah Loy, the Kapitan Cina of early Kuala Lumpur, is remembered for rebuilding the town after fire and floods devastated it in 1881. Soon afterwards, the British required new buildings to use brick and tile instead of wood and attap." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Malaysia's first railway opened in June 1885, running about 13 kilometres from Taiping to Port Weld, now called Kuala Sepetang. It was built to carry tin from the mines of Larut down to the coast for shipping." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "One of the oldest rubber trees in Malaysia still stands by a roadside in Kuala Kangsar, Perak, grown from seedlings planted in 1877. The rubber tree is native to Brazil, yet within a few decades Malaya had become one of the world's great rubber producers." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "For much of the late 1800s and 1900s, Malaya produced a large share of the world's tin. Towns such as Ipoh, Taiping and Kuala Lumpur grew up around tin mining, and old mining pools still dot the Kinta Valley today." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "In 1841, the English adventurer James Brooke became Rajah of Sarawak. He and his successors, known as the White Rajahs, ruled the territory for about a century, until 1946." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Before it was called Sabah, the territory was known as North Borneo and was administered from 1881 by the British North Borneo Chartered Company. Unusually, a private company ran the territory for more than six decades." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "The Malaysian flag was designed by Mohamed Hamzah, a young architect with the Johor Public Works Department, whose entry won a national competition. Its 14 stripes and 14-pointed star represent the 13 states and the federal government, and in 1997 it was officially named Jalur Gemilang, 'Stripes of Glory'." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "The melody of Negaraku was once the state anthem of Perak, and the same tune was known across the region through a popular song called 'Terang Bulan'. It was chosen in 1957 from among the anthems of the Malay states to become the new nation's anthem." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "The Ming admiral Zheng He, also known as Cheng Ho, called at Melaka several times in the early 1400s with his enormous treasure fleets. His visits strengthened ties between Melaka and China, and he is still fondly remembered in Melaka today." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "From 1826, the British governed Penang, Melaka and Singapore together as the Straits Settlements. In the 1890s, Perak, Selangor, Negeri Sembilan and Pahang were grouped as the Federated Malay States, with Kuala Lumpur as their capital." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "The Rukun Negara, Malaysia's national philosophy, was proclaimed in 1970 to bring Malaysians together. Generations of schoolchildren have recited its five principles at school assemblies, and they are printed on the back of many exercise books." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Labuan became a British colony in the 1840s and was prized for the coal found on the island. Steamships plying the route between Singapore and China could stop there to refuel." },
  { cat: "Sejarah", t: "Malaysia's first satellite, MEASAT-1, was launched from French Guiana in January 1996. It ushered in a new era of satellite television and telecommunications for the country." },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------- Alam
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The rafflesia has no leaves, stems or roots of its own; it lives hidden inside a jungle vine until a huge bud bursts into bloom. Some species grow nearly a metre wide, smell of rotting meat to attract flies, and wilt after only about a week." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Taman Negara, which spans Pahang, Kelantan and Terengganu, protects a rainforest often described as more than 130 million years old. It was first gazetted in 1938 as King George V National Park and renamed Taman Negara, simply 'national park', after independence." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Deep inside Gunung Mulu National Park in Sarawak lies the Sarawak Chamber, the world's largest known cave chamber by surface area. The cavers who discovered it in 1981 found it big enough to hold several jumbo jets." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Most evenings at Mulu, huge swarms of wrinkle-lipped bats pour out of Deer Cave, one of the largest cave passages in the world. They twist across the sky like ribbons of smoke as they head off to hunt insects over the rainforest." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The Sepilok Orangutan Rehabilitation Centre near Sandakan, Sabah, opened in 1964 to care for orphaned orangutans and teach them to survive in the wild. Their name comes from the Malay 'orang hutan', meaning 'person of the forest'." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The proboscis monkey lives only on Borneo, and Bako, Sarawak's oldest national park, created in 1957, is a favourite place to spot one. Males sport big, drooping noses, earning them the playful nickname 'monyet Belanda', the Dutchman monkey." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Tigers have pride of place on Malaysia's coat of arms, where two of them stand guard on either side of the shield. Sadly, the Malayan tiger is now critically endangered, surviving in the wild mainly in the forests of Peninsular Malaysia." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Baby Malayan tapirs are born covered in stripes and spots, looking a little like striped watermelons on legs. Within months the pattern fades, and they grow into black-and-white adults that look as if they are wearing a white saddle blanket." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Along Sabah's Kinabatangan River, lucky visitors may spot herds of Bornean elephants, often called pygmy elephants. They are smaller than their mainland cousins, with rounder, baby-like faces, large ears and long tails." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Sarawak is nicknamed Bumi Kenyalang, the Land of the Hornbills, and the rhinoceros hornbill, with its upturned orange casque, is the state bird. In many Dayak traditions, the hornbill is revered as a messenger between the human and spirit worlds." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "On moonless nights along the Selangor River near Kampung Kuantan, thousands of fireflies gather on berembang trees and flash in near-perfect unison. The riverbank twinkles like strings of fairy lights, a sight that has enchanted visitors for decades." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Nepenthes rajah, a giant pitcher plant found around Mount Kinabalu, has pitchers that can hold a few litres of liquid. Mountain tree shrews come to lick its sweet nectar and leave their droppings behind, which the plant absorbs as fertiliser." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "On 28 July 1960, the hibiscus, or bunga raya, was declared Malaysia's national flower. Its bold red colour stands for courage, and its five petals are today associated with the five principles of the Rukun Negara.", on: "07-28" },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "In 2019, a climber scaled a yellow meranti tree in Sabah's Danum Valley and measured it at about 100.8 metres. Nicknamed Menara, meaning 'tower', it was hailed as the tallest tropical tree known in the world." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Malaysia's national butterfly, Rajah Brooke's birdwing, has velvety black wings slashed with bands of electric green. The naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace named it in 1855 after James Brooke, the first White Rajah of Sarawak." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "While staying in Sarawak in 1855, Alfred Russel Wallace wrote a paper now known as the 'Sarawak Law', an early step towards his theory of evolution by natural selection. Borneo's astonishing wildlife helped spark ideas that changed science." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The Matang Mangrove Forest Reserve in Perak has been carefully managed for more than a century, with sections harvested in rotation for charcoal and poles and then replanted. It is often cited as one of the best-managed mangrove forests in the world." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Sipadan, off Sabah's east coast, is Malaysia's only oceanic island, rising about 600 metres from the seabed. After exploring its reefs, the legendary diver Jacques Cousteau described it as 'an untouched piece of art'." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The Belum-Temengor rainforest in northern Perak is one of the few places where all ten hornbill species found in Malaysia can be seen. At certain times of year, hornbills fly overhead in flocks numbering in the thousands." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The sun bear of Malaysia's rainforests is the smallest bear in the world. It has an extraordinarily long tongue for slurping honey and insects, and is named for the golden patch on its chest, said to look like the rising sun." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The colugo, often called the Malayan flying lemur, is neither a lemur nor able to truly fly. Instead it glides from tree to tree on a furry membrane stretched between its neck, limbs and tail, sometimes covering impressive distances in one leap." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The tualang tree can soar past 80 metres, rising high above the rainforest canopy. Giant honeybees hang dozens of nests from its smooth branches, and honey hunters traditionally climb up in the dark of night to collect the prized tualang honey." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "At Turtle Islands Park off Sandakan, Sabah, green and hawksbill turtles come ashore to lay eggs on most nights of the year. Rangers move the eggs to protected hatcheries, then release the tiny hatchlings into the sea." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "In 2000, Kinabalu Park in Sabah and Gunung Mulu National Park in Sarawak became Malaysia's first UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Both were recognised for their outstanding natural beauty and their extraordinary variety of plants and animals." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "The Gomantong Caves in Sabah are home to swiftlets whose nests, made from their own hardened saliva, are prized for bird's nest soup. Harvesters climb rattan ladders and ropes high into the cave ceiling to collect them, a skill handed down through families." },
  { cat: "Alam", t: "Perlis State Park, along the Thai border, sits on the Nakawan Range, the longest continuous limestone range in Malaysia. Its semi-deciduous forest is the only place in the country where the stump-tailed macaque lives." },

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------- Makanan
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Nasi lemak gets its name from 'lemak', the rich creaminess of rice cooked in coconut milk with a knot of pandan leaf. Wrapped in banana leaf with sambal, ikan bilis, peanuts, cucumber and egg, it is many Malaysians' idea of the perfect breakfast." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "In December 2024, 'Breakfast Culture in Malaysia' was added to UNESCO's intangible cultural heritage list. It celebrates how Malaysians of every background share tables over nasi lemak, roti canai, dim sum and steaming cups of teh tarik." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Teh tarik, 'pulled tea', is poured back and forth between two mugs held an arm's length apart. The pulling cools the tea and whips up a frothy crown, and skilled tea-pullers can do it without spilling a drop." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Roti canai dough is stretched paper-thin, folded and slapped onto a hot griddle until flaky and golden. Its name is often linked to Chennai in India, though some trace it to the Malay word 'canai', to roll or flatten dough." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Durian is the King of Fruits, and Musang King is its most celebrated variety. It was originally called Raja Kunyit, the 'Turmeric King', for its deep yellow flesh, before being renamed after Gua Musang in Kelantan, where the original tree was found." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Penang asam laksa is a tangy, fiery bowl of thick rice noodles in a mackerel broth soured with tamarind. It is topped with shredded torch ginger flower, mint, pineapple, onion and a spoonful of sweet, sticky prawn paste called hae ko." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Anthony Bourdain, the American chef and TV host, called Sarawak laksa the 'breakfast of the gods'. Kuching's favourite comes in a coppery broth of spices, tamarind and coconut milk, crowned with prawns, shredded chicken, omelette strips and a squeeze of lime." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Laksa Johor is made with spaghetti instead of rice noodles. A popular story credits Sultan Abu Bakar of Johor, who is said to have enjoyed pasta on his travels in Europe and asked his royal cooks to use it in a local dish." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Ipoh white coffee is not made from white beans. The beans are roasted with palm-oil margarine and the coffee is served with condensed milk, giving a smooth, caramel-like taste first popularised in the old coffee shops of Ipoh." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Cendol, a bowl of shaved ice, green pandan jelly and coconut milk, owes its deep, smoky sweetness to gula Melaka. This dark palm sugar carries Melaka's name and is traditionally made from the sap of coconut palm flowers." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Bite into an onde-onde, the little green ball rolled in grated coconut, and a burst of melted gula Melaka floods your mouth. In parts of Malaysia, this much-loved kuih is also known as buah Melaka." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Kuih lapis is a steamed cake of thin, colourful layers, and generations of children have eaten it the slow way, peeling off one layer at a time. Its baked cousin, kek lapis Sarawak, comes in dazzling patterns and is a must at festive open houses in Sarawak." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Nasi kerabu from Kelantan is famous for its blue rice, coloured naturally with bunga telang, the butterfly pea flower. It is served with a heap of finely sliced fresh herbs, or kerabu, along with fish, salted egg, crackers and coconut sambal." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "On the east coast, breakfast often means nasi dagang, rice steamed with coconut milk and fenugreek and served with a rich tuna curry. Terengganu and Kelantan each have their own style, and each is sure theirs is the best." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Hinava is a Kadazan-Dusun dish of raw fish 'cooked' only by lime juice, then tossed with chilli, ginger, shallots and grated bambangan seed from a wild Bornean mango. It is a favourite at Kaamatan feasts in Sabah." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Manok pansoh is an Iban dish of chicken packed into a bamboo tube with lemongrass and tapioca leaves, then cooked over an open fire. The bamboo seals in the juices and gives the broth a gentle, smoky fragrance." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Bario rice is grown in the cool Kelabit Highlands of Sarawak, about 1,000 metres above sea level, and is prized for its fine, fragrant grains. Its name is protected as a Geographical Indication, so only rice from the area may carry it." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Sarawak produces almost all of Malaysia's pepper, and its black and white peppercorns are protected by Geographical Indication status. Pepper vines climbing tall posts on hillside farms are a familiar sight in rural Sarawak." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Perlis is famous for the Harumanis mango, whose name means 'fragrant and sweet'. Only mangoes grown in Perlis may be sold under the Harumanis name, and its short season, roughly April to June, is eagerly awaited." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Pahang's signature dish is gulai tempoyak ikan patin, river catfish simmered in a rich, tangy gravy of fermented durian. Temerloh, on the banks of the Pahang River, is especially famous for its patin." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Negeri Sembilan's masak lemak cili api is a golden gravy of coconut milk, turmeric and fiery bird's-eye chillies. It reflects the state's Minangkabau heritage and is often cooked with smoked meat, duck, fish or shellfish." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Lemang, glutinous rice cooked with coconut milk in bamboo tubes lined with banana leaf, is roasted slowly over an open fire for hours. Together with ketupat and rendang, it is one of the most beloved foods of Hari Raya." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Malaysians love Milo so much that a heaped scoop of extra powder on top earns the drink the name 'Milo dinosaur'. Many grew up queueing for free cups of Milo from the green Milo van at school sports days." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "At a South Indian banana leaf restaurant, rice, vegetables, papadam and curries are served straight onto a leaf and eaten with the right hand. When finished, many diners fold the leaf towards themselves as a quiet thank-you for a satisfying meal." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Putu mayam, delicate nests of rice-flour noodles eaten with grated coconut and brown sugar, was long sold by vendors on motorcycles with a glass case on the back. Hearing the vendor's horn down the lane was a sweet part of many childhoods." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "At Chinese New Year, families and friends gather around a platter of yee sang and toss it high with chopsticks while shouting good wishes, a custom called lo hei. The higher the toss, the greater the good fortune for the year ahead." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Festive open houses come with their own cookies: crunchy murukku for Deepavali, melt-in-the-mouth kuih bangkit for Chinese New Year, and semperit and tart nenas for Hari Raya. The cookie jars are happily shared by neighbours of every background." },
  { cat: "Makanan", t: "Ambuyat, a sticky, glue-like dish made from sago starch, is a favourite in Labuan and parts of Sabah and Sarawak. It is eaten by twirling it around a two-pronged bamboo fork called a candas and dipping it into sour, spicy sauces." },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------- Budaya
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Kaamatan, Sabah's harvest festival, is celebrated throughout May and ends with public holidays on 30 and 31 May. It honours Bambaazon, the rice spirit, and the legend of Huminodun, whose sacrifice is said to have given her people rice.", on: "05-30" },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Gawai Dayak is celebrated in Sarawak on 1 June to mark the end of the rice harvest, and was first officially celebrated in 1965. Longhouses ring with gongs and music, ngajat dancers perform, and families open their doors to guests.", on: "06-01" },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "If you grew up with Malaysia's second series coins, issued from 1989, you carried a little museum in your pocket: a rebana ubi drum on the 1 sen, a gasing on the 5 sen, congkak on the 10 sen, tepak sirih on the 20 sen and a wau bulan on the 50 sen." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The wau bulan, or moon kite, is Kelantan's most famous kite, named for its crescent-shaped tail that resembles a rising moon. Many are decorated with intricate floral cut-outs, and larger kites carry a bow-shaped hummer that drones in the wind." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "In Kelantan, gasing uri contests are won by the top that spins the longest. Expertly launched, a heavy hand-carved top can whirl for well over an hour, while in gasing pangkah players try to knock rival tops out of the ring." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Congkak is played on a boat-shaped wooden board with two rows of seven holes and a larger 'rumah', or house, at each end. With seven seeds or shells in each hole, 98 pieces are in play when the game begins." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Batu seremban is played with five small stones or cloth pouches filled with rice or sand. Players toss one into the air and scoop up the others before catching it, a test of quick hands enjoyed in school corridors and on verandahs for generations." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Silat, the Malay martial art, combines graceful movement with self-defence, and it is often performed at weddings to the beat of drums. In 2019 it was inscribed on UNESCO's list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Mak Yong, a dance-theatre from Kelantan, blends acting, singing, music and dance, and its lead roles are traditionally played by women. It was among the first Malaysian traditions recognised by UNESCO, proclaimed a masterpiece of intangible heritage in 2005." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Mek Mulung, a folk theatre from the village of Wang Tepus in Kedah, has been passed down for generations with tales of kings, princesses and shamans. In 2023 UNESCO listed it as heritage in need of urgent safeguarding." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Dondang Sayang is a Melaka tradition of love songs in which singers trade pantun verses to the sound of violin, rebana and gong. It is shared by the Malay, Baba Nyonya, Chitty and Portuguese communities, and UNESCO listed it in 2018." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Songket is a luxurious hand-woven fabric in which gold or silver threads are woven into silk or cotton to create shimmering patterns. Terengganu and Kelantan are famous for it, and Malaysia's songket was inscribed by UNESCO in 2021." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The Peranakan Chinese, or Baba Nyonya, of Melaka and Penang created a culture all their own, blending Chinese and Malay traditions. Nyonya ladies were famed for embroidered kebaya and slippers decorated with thousands of tiny glass beads, called kasut manek." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The Chitty of Melaka are Peranakan Indians descended from Tamil traders who settled in the days of the Melaka Sultanate. They speak Malay and wear sarong and kebaya, while keeping their Hindu traditions alive in Kampung Chitty." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "An Iban longhouse, or rumah panjai, houses a whole community under one roof. Each family has its own bilik, and all the doors open onto the ruai, a long shared gallery where neighbours chat, work and celebrate together." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The bunga terung, or eggplant flower, is a classic Iban tattoo usually placed on the shoulder. Traditionally it was a young man's first tattoo, marking the start of his bejalai, a journey to gain experience away from home." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The sape is a boat-shaped lute of Sarawak's Orang Ulu peoples, such as the Kenyah and Kayan. Once played mainly for ritual and healing, its soft, dreamy melodies are now heard on stages around the world." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The Murut of Sabah built the lansaran, a springy wooden dance floor that works like a trampoline. Dancers bounce together in rhythm, and the boldest try to leap high enough to touch a prize hung from the rafters." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The Bajau horsemen of Kota Belud in Sabah are often called the 'Cowboys of the East'. Dressed in brilliant costumes, they ride ponies adorned with colourful trappings at festivals and parades." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The Melanau of Mukah, Sarawak, celebrate Kaul in April to welcome their new year and appease the spirits of the sea. A highlight is the tibow, a giant swing from which daring youths launch themselves high into the air." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "The Mah Meri, an Orang Asli community of Carey Island in Selangor, are renowned for wooden masks and sculptures depicting moyang, ancestral spirits. Their carvings have earned international recognition, including UNESCO awards for handicraft excellence." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Peninsular Malaysia's Orang Asli are officially grouped into three main groups, Negrito, Senoi and Proto-Malay, with 18 subgroups in all. They include the Semai, Temiar, Jakun, Temuan, Batek and Jahai, each with its own language and customs." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "In Negeri Sembilan, many families follow adat perpatih, a Minangkabau customary system that traces descent through the mother's line. Traditionally, ancestral land passes from mother to daughter." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Labu sayong is a black clay water jug shaped like a gourd, made in the village of Sayong near Kuala Kangsar, Perak. Its porous clay keeps drinking water naturally cool, which made it a household favourite long before refrigerators." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "During Hari Raya, Chinese New Year, Deepavali, Christmas, Gawai and Kaamatan, many Malaysians hold a 'rumah terbuka', or open house. Friends, neighbours and colleagues of every background drop by to eat, chat and celebrate together." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "P. Ramlee, born in Penang on 22 March 1929, was a singer, composer, actor and film director rolled into one. He starred in more than 60 films and directed over 30, and his songs remain favourites at weddings and karaoke nights." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "On 14 April 1986, Sudirman held a free concert on Chow Kit Road in Kuala Lumpur that drew around 100,000 fans, some watching from trees and rooftops. Three years later in London, he was crowned Asia's top performer at the Asian Popular Music Awards." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Cartoonist Lat, born in a kampung near Gopeng in Perak, drew on his childhood for 'Kampung Boy', published in 1979. Its gentle humour about kampung life, school and family made it a classic loved far beyond Malaysia." },
  { cat: "Budaya", t: "Legend says the Sultan of Melaka sought the hand of Puteri Gunung Ledang, the fairy princess of Mount Ledang in Johor. She asked for a golden bridge and a silver bridge to her mountain, seven trays of mosquito hearts and a bowl of the young prince's blood, and the sultan gave up." },

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------- Geografi
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "At 11.30pm on 31 December 1981, clocks in Peninsular Malaysia jumped forward 30 minutes, so 1 January 1982 began with the whole country on the same time as Sabah and Sarawak. It is one reason the sun rises so late in Penang and Kedah.", on: "01-01" },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "On 16 April 1984, the island of Labuan, off the coast of Sabah, became Malaysia's second Federal Territory. Its name comes from the Malay word 'labuhan', meaning anchorage, a nod to its sheltered harbour.", on: "04-16" },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Mount Kinabalu in Sabah rises to about 4,095 metres, the highest point in Malaysia. Its summit, Low's Peak, is named after Hugh Low, a British colonial officer who climbed the mountain in 1851 but never reached the very top." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Gunung Tahan, about 2,187 metres high, is the tallest mountain in Peninsular Malaysia. It lies deep inside Taman Negara in Pahang, and the classic trek to its summit takes several days of jungle walking and river crossings." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "The Rajang River in Sarawak, about 565 kilometres long, is the longest river in Malaysia. For generations it has served as a highway into the interior, with express boats carrying passengers between towns such as Sibu and Kapit." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "The Pahang River, about 459 kilometres long, is the longest river in Peninsular Malaysia. It begins where the Jelai and Tembeling rivers meet and flows past Temerloh and the royal town of Pekan to the South China Sea." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "The Kinabatangan, Sabah's longest river at about 560 kilometres, is the second longest in Malaysia. Its lower reaches wind through floodplain forest, oxbow lakes and mangroves, forming one of the richest wildlife corridors in Borneo." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Tanjung Piai in Johor is the southernmost point of mainland Asia. A boardwalk leads through its mangroves to the very tip, where you can watch ships passing through the busy waters of the Strait of Melaka." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Pulau Banggi, off the northern tip of Sabah, is the largest island lying entirely within Malaysia, at about 440 square kilometres. That makes it bigger than both Langkawi and Penang Island." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Borneo is the third largest island in the world and the only one shared by three countries: Malaysia, Indonesia and Brunei. Sabah and Sarawak together occupy the island's northern part." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Sarawak is Malaysia's largest state, at about 124,450 square kilometres, almost as large as the whole of Peninsular Malaysia. Its capital, Kuching, sits on the Sarawak River, not far from the sea." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Perlis is Malaysia's smallest state, at only about 820 square kilometres. Small as it is, it shares a border with Thailand, has its own royal town at Arau, and is known for its paddy fields and limestone hills." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Sabah is nicknamed the Land Below the Wind because it lies just south of the typhoon belt. The fierce storms that sweep across the western Pacific usually pass to the north, sparing the state their full force." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "The Strait of Melaka, between Peninsular Malaysia and Sumatra, is one of the busiest shipping lanes on Earth. For centuries, whoever controlled it held a key to trade between the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "The Titiwangsa Range runs down the middle of Peninsular Malaysia like a backbone, dividing the west coast from the east. Its slopes feed many of the peninsula's rivers and host hill stations such as Cameron Highlands, Genting Highlands and Fraser's Hill." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Malaysia's weather is shaped by two monsoons: the southwest monsoon from about May to September and the northeast monsoon from about November to March. The northeast monsoon brings heavy rain to the east coast, when many islands there quieten down for the season." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Malaysia lies just a few degrees north of the equator, so days and nights stay close to 12 hours long all year round. There is no winter or summer, only wetter and drier spells, and afternoon thunderstorms are part of daily life." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Cameron Highlands in Pahang is named after William Cameron, a British surveyor who mapped the area in 1885. Lying at around 1,500 metres, its cool climate made it perfect for tea estates, strawberry farms and vegetable plots." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Kedah is known as Jelapang Padi, the rice bowl of Malaysia, and its flat plains turn from green to gold as the paddy ripens. Together with neighbouring Perlis, it produces a large share of the country's rice." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Penang Hill rises to about 833 metres at Western Hill, its highest point. Since 1923, a funicular railway has carried visitors up from the hot lowlands to its cooler air, and it is the only funicular railway in Malaysia." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Legend says Pulau Tioman in Pahang was once a dragon princess who, while flying to meet her prince, stopped to rest in the calm South China Sea and decided to stay. Her body became the island, and its twin peaks are said to be her horns." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Tasik Chini in Pahang is a chain of about a dozen connected lakes, famous for carpets of blooming lotus. Folklore of the local Jakun community speaks of a mythical dragon, Sri Gumum, said to dwell beneath its waters." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "The Langkawi archipelago in Kedah, made up of about 99 islands, became Southeast Asia's first UNESCO Global Geopark in 2007. Rocks in its Machinchang range are among the oldest in Malaysia, formed hundreds of millions of years ago." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Tasik Bera in Pahang became Malaysia's first Ramsar wetland of international importance in 1994. It is the country's largest natural freshwater lake system, and the Semelai, an Orang Asli people, have lived along its waters for generations." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "Tasik Kenyir in Terengganu, created in 1985 when the Kenyir River was dammed, is one of the largest man-made lakes in Southeast Asia. As the water rose, around 340 hilltops were left poking out as islands." },
  { cat: "Geografi", t: "The Maliau Basin in Sabah, a nearly circular, saucer-shaped basin about 25 kilometres across, is often called Sabah's Lost World. Ringed by steep cliffs, it stayed largely unexplored until surprisingly recent times." },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------- Bahasa
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Dewan Bahasa dan Pustaka began on 22 June 1956 in Johor Bahru as a small office called Balai Pustaka. It moved to Kuala Lumpur in 1957 and has since shaped Malay dictionaries, spelling and countless textbooks.", on: "06-22" },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "On 16 August 1972, Malaysia and Indonesia introduced a shared new spelling system. That is why vintage signs may say 'chakap' or 'chantek', while today we write 'cakap' and 'cantik'." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Kuala Lumpur means 'muddy confluence' in Malay. A 'kuala' is where two rivers meet or where a river flows into the sea, and the Klang and Gombak rivers carried plenty of mud, especially once tin mining began upstream." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Legend says Parameswara was resting under a tree when one of his hunting dogs was kicked into the river by a plucky little mouse deer. Taking it as a good omen, he founded his city on the spot and named it after the tree: Melaka." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Pulau Pinang means 'island of the areca nut palm', the pinang tree whose nut is chewed with betel leaf in the traditional sirih custom. The palm still stands proudly on the Penang state flag." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Negeri Sembilan means 'nine states', recalling the nine territories, or luak, that once formed its confederation. Uniquely, its ruler is known as the Yang di-Pertuan Besar rather than Sultan." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Perak means 'silver' in Malay, and a popular explanation is that the name was inspired by the silvery gleam of tin, which the state had in abundance. Tin made Perak one of the richest corners of Malaya." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Johor's name is often traced to the Arabic word 'jauhar', meaning jewel or precious stone, said to be what Arab traders called the area. Its capital, Johor Bahru, simply means 'new Johor'." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "A popular tale says Sarawak's name comes from 'saya serah pada awak', or 'I hand it over to you'. In fact the name is older, and is generally believed to come from 'serawak', a local word for antimony, an ore once mined there." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Mount Kinabalu's name is often traced to the Kadazan-Dusun 'Aki Nabalu', the revered place of the dead. Many communities traditionally believe the spirits of their ancestors rest upon the mountain." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Kota Kinabalu was once called Jesselton, after Sir Charles Jessel of the British North Borneo Chartered Company. On 30 September 1968, it was renamed after the great mountain that watches over the city." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Ipoh is named after the pokok ipoh, a tree whose poisonous sap was traditionally used on blowpipe darts by Orang Asli hunters. The city itself grew rich on tin from the surrounding Kinta Valley." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Alor Setar joins 'alor', a small stream, with 'setar', a tree bearing small, sour, mango-like fruit that once grew along its rivers. Older maps and signs spell it Alor Star, and both spellings are still seen." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Batu Pahat in Johor means 'chiselled stone'. Legend says that in the 15th century, retreating Siamese soldiers chipped away at the rocks on the shore in search of fresh water, and a well said to be the result can still be visited." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Putrajaya takes its name from Tunku Abdul Rahman Putra Al-Haj, Malaysia's first prime minister. 'Putra' means prince or son and 'jaya' means success or victory, both words that came into Malay from Sanskrit." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Many everyday Malay words arrived with the Portuguese after 1511: bendera (flag) from bandeira, almari (cupboard) from armário, garpu (fork) from garfo, jendela (window) from janela and keju (cheese) from queijo. Even sekolah comes from escola." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Malay is a wonderfully well-travelled language. 'Kedai' (shop) and 'kapal' (ship) are linked to Tamil, 'teh' and 'kuih' come from Hokkien, and grand words like 'bahasa', 'negara' and 'raja' arrived from Sanskrit centuries ago." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "English has borrowed from Malay too. 'Amok' comes from 'amuk', the 'compound' around a house is traced to 'kampung', and words such as 'rattan', 'paddy', 'gong' and 'sarong' all have Malay roots." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Malay happily borrows from English and spells the words just as they sound: bas, teksi, polis, basikal, kek and aiskrim. Read 'stesen' or 'televisyen' aloud and you will recognise them at once." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "'Kopitiam' blends the Malay 'kopi' with the Hokkien 'tiam', meaning shop. Plain kopi comes with condensed milk, kopi O is black with sugar, kopi C has evaporated milk, 'kosong' means no sugar and 'peng' means iced." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "Malaysians head to the 'mamak' for late-night roti canai and teh tarik. The word, used for Indian-Muslim eateries and their owners, is usually traced to the Tamil word for uncle, fitting for places where everyone is treated like family." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "A pantun is a four-line Malay verse that rhymes a-b-a-b. The first two lines, the pembayang, paint a picture from nature, while the last two carry the real message, and in 2020 pantun joined UNESCO's intangible heritage list." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "The Malay proverb 'bagai katak di bawah tempurung', like a frog under a coconut shell, describes someone with a narrow view of the world. Stuck under its shell, the frog believes the inside of the shell is the whole sky." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "'Ringgit' is an old Malay word meaning jagged, a nod to the serrated edges of the Spanish silver dollars that once circulated in the region. The smaller unit, 'sen', comes from the English 'cent'." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "The rambutan gets its name from the Malay word 'rambut', meaning hair, thanks to its soft, hairy red skin. The word has travelled the world, and the fruit is called rambutan in many languages, including English." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "In Melaka's Portuguese Settlement, some families still speak Kristang, a creole that grew from 16th-century Portuguese mixed with Malay. It is one of the last living Portuguese-based creoles in Asia and is considered endangered." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "At Kaamatan in Sabah, friends greet each other with 'Kotobian Tadau Tagazo do Kaamatan', wishing a happy harvest festival in Kadazan-Dusun. At Gawai in Sarawak, the Iban wish is 'gayu guru, gerai nyamai', for long life, good health and prosperity." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "A beach near Kota Bharu, Kelantan, was once called Pantai Cinta Berahi, the Beach of Passionate Love. It was later renamed Pantai Cahaya Bulan, Moonlight Beach, conveniently keeping the old initials PCB." },
  { cat: "Bahasa", t: "The Perhentian Islands off Terengganu are said to take their name from 'perhentian', a stopping place, because traders sailing between the peninsula and Bangkok would pause there to rest and take on supplies." },

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------- Sukan
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "On 23 May 1997, M. Magendran stood on the summit of Mount Everest, becoming the first Malaysian to reach the top of the world. His teammate N. Mohanadas followed him to the top shortly after.", on: "05-23" },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Malaya won the very first Thomas Cup, the world men's team badminton championship, beating Denmark 8-1 in the 1949 final in Preston, England. It was the start of a lasting love affair between Malaysians and the shuttlecock." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "In 1992, Malaysia won the Thomas Cup again after a long wait, beating Indonesia 3-2 in a nail-biting final at Stadium Negara in Kuala Lumpur. The victory sparked joyful celebrations around the country." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Malaysia's first Olympic medal came at Barcelona 1992, when brothers Razif and Jalani Sidek won bronze in the men's badminton doubles. It was the very first Olympics to award medals in badminton." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "The Sidek brothers were famous for the 'S-serve', a spinning serve that made the shuttle wobble and swerve unpredictably. It proved so hard to return that the sport's governing body eventually banned it." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Lee Chong Wei won three Olympic silver medals in men's singles badminton, at Beijing 2008, London 2012 and Rio 2016. He spent years ranked world number one and inspired a whole generation of young Malaysian players." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "At the London 2012 Olympics, diver Pandelela Rinong, from a Bidayuh village in Bau, Sarawak, won bronze in the 10m platform. She became the first Malaysian woman to win an Olympic medal, and the first Malaysian to medal in a sport other than badminton." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Malaysia won its first-ever Paralympic gold medals at Rio 2016, all three in athletics: Mohamad Ridzuan Puzi in the 100m, Muhammad Ziyad Zolkefli in the shot put and Abdul Latif Romly in the long jump." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Penang-born squash legend Nicol David was ranked women's world number one for a record 108 consecutive months. She won the World Open title eight times, more than any other woman in the history of the sport." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Track cyclist Azizulhasni Awang from Terengganu is nicknamed the 'Pocket Rocketman' for his small build and explosive speed. He won Olympic keirin medals at two Games: bronze at Rio 2016 and silver at Tokyo 2020." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "The Malaysia Cup began in 1921 as the Malaya Cup, played for a trophy donated by the crew of the British battleship HMS Malaya. It is one of the oldest football competitions in Asia, and its finals still draw roaring crowds." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Malaysia's football team qualified for the Olympic Games twice, for Munich 1972 and Moscow 1980, though Malaysia joined the boycott of the Moscow Games. That golden era produced legends such as Mokhtar Dahari, fondly known as SuperMokh." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "In 2010, Malaysia won the AFF Suzuki Cup, Southeast Asia's football championship, for the first time, beating Indonesia over two legs in the final. The home leg at Bukit Jalil National Stadium was a sea of yellow." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "In 1998, Kuala Lumpur hosted the Commonwealth Games, the first time the event was held in Asia. Those Games were also the first to include team sports, among them hockey, netball, cricket and rugby sevens." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "When Kuala Lumpur hosted the 1975 Hockey World Cup, Malaysia's team reached the semi-finals, losing narrowly to India in extra time. Their fourth-place finish is still fondly remembered as a high point of Malaysian hockey." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "The Sepang International Circuit in Selangor hosted the Formula One Malaysian Grand Prix from 1999 to 2017. Designed by Hermann Tilke, its long straights, tight corners and sudden tropical downpours made it one of the toughest races on the calendar." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Alex Yoong became the first Malaysian to race in Formula One, making his debut with the Minardi team in 2001. The following year, he lined up on the grid at his home Grand Prix in Sepang." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Sepak takraw combines the Malay word 'sepak', to kick, with the Thai 'takraw', a woven ball. Players use their feet, knees, chest and head, never their hands, to send the ball over the net with spectacular overhead kicks." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "In July 1975, boxing legend Muhammad Ali defended his world heavyweight title against Joe Bugner at Stadium Merdeka in Kuala Lumpur. Ali won on points after 15 rounds fought in the tropical heat." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "In 1999, Azhar Mansor became the first Malaysian to sail solo around the world, aboard a yacht named Jalur Gemilang. His voyage, including stops along the way, took about 190 days." },
  { cat: "Sukan", t: "Le Tour de Langkawi, first held in 1996, is Malaysia's best-known professional cycling race. Riders speed past paddy fields and seaside towns, and the punishing climb to Genting Highlands has long been one of its most dramatic stages." },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------- Tempat
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "On 7 July 2008, Melaka and George Town were jointly inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the Historic Cities of the Straits of Malacca. Together they tell the story of some 500 years of trade and cultural exchange between East and West.", on: "07-07" },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Penang Bridge opened on 14 September 1985, linking Penang Island to Seberang Perai on the mainland. Stretching about 13.5 kilometres, it ended the days when the ferry was the only way to cross the channel.", on: "09-14" },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Petronas Twin Towers in Kuala Lumpur, about 452 metres tall, were the world's tallest buildings from 1998 to 2004. Their floor plan is based on an eight-pointed star from Islamic geometry, and a double-decker skybridge links them at the 41st and 42nd floors." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "To reach the temple cave at Batu Caves in Selangor, visitors climb 272 steps, repainted in rainbow colours in 2018. At the foot stands a golden statue of Lord Murugan, about 42.7 metres tall, and at Thaipusam huge crowds of devotees make the climb." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Sultan Abdul Samad Building, with its gleaming copper domes and clock tower, was completed in 1897 facing what is now Dataran Merdeka. On the field before it, the Union Jack was lowered at midnight as 31 August 1957 began." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Architect Arthur Benison Hubback gave Malaysia some of its most romantic landmarks, including Masjid Jamek, where the Klang and Gombak rivers meet, and the Moorish-style Kuala Lumpur Railway Station. He also designed Ipoh's grand railway station, nicknamed the 'Taj Mahal of Ipoh'." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The National Monument, or Tugu Negara, in Kuala Lumpur was sculpted by Felix de Weldon, the American sculptor behind the famous Iwo Jima memorial in the United States. Unveiled in 1966, its bronze soldiers honour those who gave their lives for the nation's peace and freedom." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Putrajaya, Malaysia's planned administrative capital, was built on former plantation land about 25 kilometres south of Kuala Lumpur. Its pink-domed Putra Mosque, built with rose-tinted granite, seems to float beside a vast man-made lake." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Kek Lok Si in Air Itam, Penang, is the largest Buddhist temple complex in Malaysia, built in stages from 1890. Its Pagoda of Ten Thousand Buddhas has a Chinese-style base, a Thai-style middle and a Burmese-style crown." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "In 2012, Lithuanian artist Ernest Zacharevic painted two children riding a real bicycle on a wall in George Town. 'Little Children on a Bicycle' became an instant icon and helped turn Penang's streets into an open-air art gallery." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Melaka's Jalan Tokong is nicknamed Harmony Street. Within a short stroll stand Cheng Hoon Teng, the oldest functioning Chinese temple in Malaysia, the Kampung Kling Mosque and Sri Poyyatha Vinayagar Moorthi Temple, one of the oldest Hindu temples in the country." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "All that remains of A Famosa, the fortress the Portuguese built in Melaka after 1511, is a single gate, the Porta de Santiago. It survived because Sir Stamford Raffles stepped in during the early 1800s to stop the last of it being torn down." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Kellie's Castle near Batu Gajah, Perak, was the dream home of Scottish planter William Kellie Smith. When he died suddenly on a trip to Lisbon in 1926, work stopped, leaving an unfinished mansion that is now a magnet for ghost stories." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Leaning Tower of Teluk Intan in Perak was built in 1885 as a water tank and clock tower. Soft ground made it tilt, earning comparisons with Pisa, and the town itself was known as Teluk Anson until it was renamed in 1982." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Taiping in Perak is home to the Perak Museum, opened in 1883 and the oldest museum in Malaysia, and the Taiping Lake Gardens, created around 1880 on old tin-mining land. Giant rain trees arch over the lakeside roads like a green tunnel." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Istana Lama Seri Menanti in Negeri Sembilan is a four-storey wooden palace built between 1902 and 1908 without a single nail. It stands on 99 timber pillars, said to represent the warriors of the state's clans." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Masjid Kampung Laut in Kelantan is one of the oldest mosques in Malaysia, built of chengal wood without nails. After repeated floods it was dismantled and moved to Nilam Puri, and it has since been returned to its home village near the river." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Pasar Siti Khadijah in Kota Bharu, Kelantan, is famous for its traders, most of whom are women. Its bustling, colourful central hall is piled high with fresh produce, kuih and local specialities." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Crystal Mosque in Kuala Terengganu, built of steel, glass and crystal, sparkles on Wan Man Island beside the Terengganu River. At night its lights shimmer across the water, making it one of the state's best-loved sights." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Pekan Rabu in Alor Setar means 'Wednesday Market', because it began as an open-air market held only on Wednesdays. It grew into a busy bazaar famous for dodol, crackers, songkok and traditional clothes." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Gua Kelam in Perlis, whose name means 'dark cave', is a passage of about 370 metres through a limestone hill once used by tin miners. Visitors follow a suspended walkway above a rushing underground stream to the other side." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Sultan Salahuddin Abdul Aziz Mosque in Shah Alam, nicknamed the Blue Mosque for its huge blue-and-silver dome, is the largest mosque in Malaysia. Its four minarets, about 142 metres tall, were once listed as the tallest in the world." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Johor-Singapore Causeway, just over a kilometre long, was officially opened in 1924. For decades it was the only land link across the Strait of Johor, carrying cars, trains and even water pipelines." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Fraser's Hill in Pahang is named after Louis James Fraser, a Scottish adventurer who is said to have traded tin there in the late 1800s before mysteriously disappearing. Today the cool hill station is loved for its mossy forests, stone cottages and birdwatching." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Atkinson Clock Tower, completed in 1905, is the oldest standing structure in Kota Kinabalu. Built of timber in memory of Jesselton's first district officer, it was one of the few buildings to survive the bombing of the town during World War II." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "Fort Margherita in Kuching was built in 1879 by Charles Brooke, the second White Rajah, and named after his wife, Ranee Margaret. Across the Sarawak River stands the Astana, the palace he built in 1870 as a wedding gift for her." },
  { cat: "Tempat", t: "The Labuan Chimney, a red-brick tower about 106 feet tall built in the 1800s, has puzzled historians for generations. It may have been linked to the island's coal mining, yet investigations have found no trace of soot inside." },
];
